	<Fragment slot="hero-title">REBUILDING WITHOUT ERASING WHAT WAS ALREADY THERE</Fragment>
	<div class="article-reconstruction">
		<p>Today I opened one of those demos I have been carrying around for years, and it made me think about something that keeps coming back during this process: finishing a song and rebuilding one are not exactly the same thing.</p>
		<p>Finishing something can mean continuing from wherever I left it. Rebuilding means going back, looking at what is already there and deciding what still makes sense to keep, what needs to change, and what was probably there simply because, at the time, I did not know how to do it differently.</p>
		<p>That is something I am constantly doing with Fhugawz.</p>
		<p>There are songs that started years ago, ideas that belong to completely different periods of my life, demos that no longer represent how I want the records to sound, and decisions I would probably make differently today. I still keep returning to them because there is usually something there that continues to interest me.</p>
		<p>I do not want to preserve them exactly as they were. But I also do not want to lose whatever made me care about them in the first place.</p>

		<h2>I do not always want to start from zero</h2>
		<p>For a long time I associated improving a song with replacing things: changing the drums, recording guitars again, finding better sounds, rearranging the structure or cleaning up the mix. I will probably continue doing many of those things because I am also learning more about production while I build these records.</p>
		<p>But the more I return to old material, the more I realize that starting completely from zero is not always the best decision.</p>
		<p>A demo can be badly recorded and still contain an atmosphere that becomes surprisingly difficult to recreate later. A performance can be imperfect but communicate something that disappears when I try to perform it “better.” Even a strange sound that happened by accident can eventually become one of the details I remember most from the song.</p>
		<p>Now I am trying to listen to those early versions differently, not only looking for what is wrong but trying to recognize what should not disappear.</p>
		<p>That changes the process quite a bit.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/sound-of-reconstruction/sound-of-reconstruction-ai-demo-to-human-production.webp" alt="An early song demo being revisited during the reconstruction process." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>Imperfection contains information too</h2>
		<p>Incomplete recordings are interesting because many of the decisions inside them have not passed through too many filters yet.</p>
		<p>A guitar enters where I felt it should enter, a vocal ended up slightly behind, a silence lasts longer than expected, or some unplanned noise appears and completely changes the feeling of a section. If it works for me, I might keep it. If it starts feeling like an accident that distracts from everything else, I will probably change it. I do not need to turn every mistake into something valuable just because it happened.</p>
		<p>I am also learning to distinguish between an imperfect decision and a decision I simply do not know how to solve yet.</p>
		<p>That is not always obvious.</p>
		<p>Sometimes I listen to a demo and immediately know what I want to correct. Other times I prefer to leave it alone for a few days because I still do not really understand what is bothering me. Before, I probably would have started moving things immediately. Now I am trying to listen a little longer before doing that.</p>

		<h2>Rebuilding also forces me to understand each song better</h2>
		<p>When I return to an old demo, I try not to begin by immediately changing sounds.</p>
		<p>First I need to remember what interested me about that song.</p>
		<p>It could be a melody, a progression, a particular way of singing a phrase, a texture or simply the overall feeling I had when I made it. Sometimes I find that center very quickly. Other times I discover that the thing I thought was important was not actually what was holding the song together.</p>
		<p>That is where the process becomes interesting to me.</p>
		<p>I can change quite a lot around that center: structure, instruments, performances and even complete parts of the production. But if I also remove the thing that made me want to return to the song, I am probably building something else.</p>
		<p>And that is not necessarily a problem either. I just want to notice when it happens.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/sound-of-reconstruction/sound-of-reconstruction-music-production-session.webp" alt="A music production session focused on understanding and rebuilding an older song." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>Everything starts connecting to the Worlds</h2>
		<p>One of the things that has changed the way I work the most is that I no longer think of every song as a completely isolated piece.</p>
		<p>There is a World around it now.</p>
		<p>That means some decisions that could work perfectly well inside a song stop working once I hear it as part of an album. A sound can feel too clean, a drum too modern, a performance too controlled or a production too large for the narrative space where I want the song to exist.</p>
		<p>So I sometimes make decisions I probably would not make if I were simply trying to create the “best possible version” of every track independently.</p>
		<p>I care more about whether it belongs to the right world.</p>
		<p>That also forces me to learn quite a lot. I am understanding more about production precisely because I keep finding specific problems I want to solve inside the project: how to make something feel more intimate without simply turning everything down, how to make a sound feel damaged without turning that damage into an obvious effect, or how to make very different albums still feel like parts of the same sequence.</p>
		<p>A lot of the time I do not know the answer when I start.</p>
		<p>I find it by working.</p>

		<h2>I do not want to preserve the past intact</h2>
		<p>Fhugawz begins in memory, but I do not want that memory to become a museum.</p>
		<p>I am not interested in keeping every song exactly as it was originally imagined, and in some cases I do not think that would even be possible. I am not the same person who wrote many of them either.</p>
		<p>When I return to them now, I hear different things. Some lyrics mean something else, decisions that once seemed right no longer convince me, and details I probably would have removed years ago now feel interesting.</p>
		<p>That is why I have started thinking about reconstruction more like a conversation with those earlier versions than an attempt to restore them.</p>
		<p>Memory does not remain intact either. Every time we return to something, we reinterpret part of it, even when we believe we remember it exactly as it was.</p>
		<p>Something similar is happening with these songs.</p>

		<div class="article-image">
			<img src="/images/blog/sound-of-reconstruction/sound-of-reconstruction-stems-mixing-process.webp" alt="Song stems and arrangement details being reconsidered during reconstruction." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
		</div>

		<h2>What deserves to stay</h2>
		<p>I suppose the process eventually comes down to recognizing what needs to survive for each song to remain emotionally recognizable. It could be a melody, a phrase, a noise behind the voice, or that feeling I still do not really know how to describe. Everything else can change, and a lot of it probably will.</p>
		<p>There will also be songs where I get it wrong.</p>
		<p>I might rebuild something for weeks and eventually realize that the first demo had an energy I cannot recover anymore. A new version might be technically better and I may still like it less. Those situations are also part of what I want to document here because, at least right now, I do not see much value in pretending that every decision necessarily leads to an improvement.</p>
		<p>I am learning while I build these records.</p>
		<p>And maybe that is why reconstruction has become such an important part of this project. It is not about returning the songs exactly to where they were. It is about understanding what still makes sense after taking them apart and looking at them again from where I am now.</p>
	</div>
</ArticleLayout>
